  "answer": "The bug in the provided C# method is that the loop condition is incorrect. The loop should iterate from \u00600\u0060 to \u0060values.Length - 1\u0060, but the current condition \u0060i \u003C= values.Length\u0060 will cause an \u0060IndexOutOfRangeException\u0060 when \u0060i\u0060 equals \u0060values.Length\u0060.\n\nTo fix this, change the loop condition to \u0060i \u003C values.Length\u0060.\n\nHere is the minimal fix:\n\n\u0060\u0060\u0060csharp\nstatic int Sum(int[] values)\n{\n    int total = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; i \u003C values.Length; i\u002B\u002B) total \u002B= values[i];\n    return total;\n}\n\u0060\u0060\u0060\n\n### Explanation of Empty-Array Behavior\n\nWhen the \u0060values\u0060 array is empty (\u0060values.Length == 0\u0060), the loop condition \u0060i \u003C values.Length\u0060 will immediately evaluate to \u0060false\u0060 because \u0060i\u0060 starts at \u00600\u0060 and \u0060values.Length\u0060 is \u00600\u0060. Therefore, the loop body will not execute at all.\n\nAs a result, the \u0060total\u0060 variable, which is initialized to \u00600\u0060, will remain \u00600\u0060 and will be returned by the method. This is the correct behavior for an empty array, as the sum of no numbers should be \u00600\u0060.",
